    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.strong_product - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.strong_product.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.strong_product()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute the strong product of this graph with another.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.strong_product()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute the strong product of this graph with another.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.strong_product()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Compute the strong product of this graph with another.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCompute the strong product of this graph with another.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L51756-L51839){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.strong_product(other)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Strong product](../assets/graphs/strong-product.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"G is a path a–b–c (accent edges) and H a path 1–2–3 (teal). Their strong product is the 3 by 3 king's graph, labeled a1 to c3: accent edges along the rows copy G, teal edges down the columns copy H, and ink diagonals move both coordinates at once. It has 9 nodes and 20 edges, and the center (b, 2), outlined, reaches all 8 others.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nThe strong product $G \\boxtimes H$ is the union of the Cartesian and tensor products:\n`(g, h)` and `(g', h')` are joined when each coordinate either stays put or moves along\nan edge, and at least one moves. A path times a path is a grid with its diagonals, the\n\"king's graph\" of chess, so the strong product models moves that may change several\ncoordinates at once, and its graph distance is the maximum (Chebyshev) of the factor\ndistances. It appears in information theory: the Shannon capacity of a channel is\ndefined through strong powers of its confusability graph.\n\nBoth graphs must be directed or both undirected. Edges from the Cartesian part keep the\nweight and attributes of their factor edge; edges from the tensor part get the product\nof the two weights.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[other]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The second factor. Neither graph is modified.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A new graph with `n * m` nodes, numbered and labeled as in `cartesian_product()`.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If one graph is directed and the other is not.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nThe product has $n|E_H| + m|E_G| + 2|E_G||E_H|$ edges when undirected and simple, and\n$d((g, h), (g', h')) = \\max(d_G(g, g'), d_H(h, h'))$. When both factors are simple the\nproduct is simple, matching NetworkX's `strong_product`.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~cartesian_product`) \n\n:   Join pairs that move in one graph at a time.\n\n[](`~tensor_product`) \n\n:   Join pairs that move in both graphs at once.\n\n[](`~lexicographic_product`) \n\n:   Replace each node with a copy of another graph.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe strong product has the edges of both the Cartesian and the tensor product. Two paths\nof three give the moves of a king on a 3 x 3 board. The center square reaches all 8 others\nin one move and a corner reaches 3:\n\n::: {#3375fab4 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\nfiles = ec.Graph().add_nodes([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])\nfiles.add_edges([(\"a\", \"b\"), (\"b\", \"c\")])\nranks = ec.Graph().add_nodes([\"1\", \"2\", \"3\"])\nranks.add_edges([(\"1\", \"2\"), (\"2\", \"3\")])\n\nkings = files.strong_product(ranks)\nkings.get_degree(\"(b, 2)\"), kings.get_degree(\"(a, 1)\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(8, 3)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nPlacing each square by its label lays out the board, with the center's moves highlighted:\n\n::: {#11d5ae3c .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nsquares = kings.get_node_df()\nboard = {\n    n: (\"abc\".index(label[1]), int(label[-2]))\n    for n, label in zip(squares[\"id\"], squares[\"label\"])\n}\ncenter = kings.get_id_from_label(\"(b, 2)\")\nmoves = kings.get_neighbors(center)\n\nkings.set_node_attrs(\"pos\", board).plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", label_by=\"label\", highlight=[center] + moves,\n    highlight_edges=[(center, n) for n in moves],\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"700\" height=\"500\" viewBox=\"5.6 14.0 688.8 492.0\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;\">\n<rect x=\"5.6\" y=\"14.0\" width=\"688.8\" height=\"492.0\" fill=\"var(--ect-3239c383)\"/>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"424.0\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"266.0\" stroke=\"var(--ect-e5dfd507)\" stroke-width=\"2.46\" stroke-opacity=\"0.2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n<line x1=\"540.0\" y1=\"424.0\" x2=\"540.0\" y2=\"266.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"transparent\" stroke-width=\"8.0\" pointer-events=\"stroke\" data-edge=\"7-8-0\" data-attrs='